   {
    "text": "**The Ball of Tennis, in Medieval Style**\n\nIn ancient courts, where knights and lords did play,  \nA ball of leather, soft and bright, was laid.  \nWith feathers, spun from threads of fine and rare,  \nIt danced with grace, and sparks did arise.  \n\nThe court was round, and the players stood,  \nWith sticks of oak, and gloves of wool, they'd go.  \nThe ball, though small, was mighty in its flight,  \nA test of skill, and strength, and might.  \n\nIn realms of sport, where nobles did compete,  \nA serve was swift, and a stroke was true.  \nThrough tournaments, where the champions stood,  \nThe ball was tossed, and the game was done.  \n\nThe realm of tennis, vast and wide,  \nWith every swing, a tale was told.  \nFrom court to court, the ball was passed,  \nA symbol of the game, both old and new.  \n\nP.P.S. — Though modern rules differ, the ball remains,  \nA symbol of the game, both old and new.",
